  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m does "persists across sessions" mean [3mthis browser on this machine remembers[0m (client-side), or [3mthis user's [0m
  [3maccount remembers, on any device[0m (server-side)? The repo is genuinely ambiguous — it has a browser app and a Node
  entry point that don't talk to each other, and the login is a stub returning [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m without authenticating
  anyone.

  - [1mBrowser-local ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys you: works today, zero dependencies, zero backend, preferences survive tab
    closes and restarts. Costs: per-browser, per-device; lost when cache is cleared; not secret from the user. Rules
    out: cross-device sync without a later rewrite of the storage layer.
  - [1mServer-side, per account[0m — buys you: real cross-device persistence, one source of truth. Costs: needs a real
    backend, a real database, and above all [3mreal authentication[0m — the current [38;5;153mlogin()[39m doesn't authenticate, so there's
    no identity to key preferences to. That's a much larger project than the one you asked for. Rules out: nothing, but
    it's a big step up in scope.
  - [1mLocal-first with a sync seam[0m — buys you: ships as localStorage now, behind an interface a server backend can
    implement later. Costs: a little indirection you don't need on day one.

  [1mMy recommendation: local-first with a sync seam.[0m Server-side is the honest answer to "any device," but it requires
  building authentication first, and I don't want to silently turn "add preferences storage" into "build an auth system
  and a backend." Local-first gets you working persistence immediately, and the seam means choosing the server later is
  a new implementation of one interface rather than a rewrite of every call site. If you know you'll never need
  cross-device, plain localStorage is fine and I'll drop the indirection.
